The '''noosphere''' is a historical philosophical and scientific concept referring to the sphere or planetary influence of human thought, knowledge and activity. Its early development involved Vladimir Vernadsky, Édouard Le Roy and Pierre Teilhard de Chardin.

== Intellectual history ==
The concept emerged from intellectual exchanges in the 1920s around Vernadsky's biosphere research, Le Roy's philosophy and Teilhard's evolutionary thought. Vernadsky's 1945 essay considers the changing geological significance of human activity. Teilhard developed a teleological account of evolution and collective thought. The historical review by Vidal et al. discusses competing planetary-superorganism and evolutionary-transition interpretations; these are not automatically verified by the historical record.

== Material and cognitive claims ==
Physical effects can be measured through land use, energy throughput, carbon flux and material extraction. Information-mediated coordination can be investigated through communication networks, institutions and knowledge diffusion. Neither measurement establishes the existence of a literal sphere of thought or a unified conscious subject. The noosphere can function as a historical framework, research metaphor or philosophical hypothesis; identify which interpretation is being evaluated.

== Contemporary use and limitations ==
A study might compare rates of environmental change associated with specific technologies and institutional decisions, using independently measured flows and causal analysis. A study of collective knowledge would require different data and success criteria. Distinguish the noosphere from the infosphere and global brain: the former has a historical planetary and evolutionary focus, while the latter concepts emphasize informational environments or networked processing. None implies an inevitable direction of human evolution.

